SAMPLES/INIT - start diablo and dreaderd with the init system of the machine

These are thin wrappers: every one calls samples/adm/diablo-ctl (installed as
/news/adm/diablo-ctl), which does the starting, stopping, status and checks,
reads its settings from diablo-ctl.conf (see samples/adm/diablo-ctl.conf) and
only ever touches its own site.  Edit the options of the daemons there (-p
pathhost and so on), never in a wrapper.

The wrappers assume the install prefix /news.  With another PREFIX replace
/news/adm/diablo-ctl (and /news/log/*.pid in the systemd units) by yours, and
give diablo-ctl "-C <prefix>/diablo.config" (put it into the ExecStart and
ExecStop lines, the SMF exec attributes, diablo_flags in rc.conf, or
DIABLO_CTL_OPTS): the programs read /news/diablo.config unless they are told
another, and that file needs "path_home <prefix>".  Without -C a second site's
wrapper would start, stop and check the site in /news.

  systemd/diablo.service, dreaderd.service
		Linux with systemd: copy to /etc/systemd/system, then
		    systemctl daemon-reload
		    systemctl enable --now diablo       (and/or dreaderd)
		Use "systemctl edit" for a spool on its own file system:
		RequiresMountsFor=/news/spool

  freebsd/diablo, dreaderd
		FreeBSD rc.d: copy to /usr/local/etc/rc.d (mode 555), then
		    sysrc diablo_enable=YES dreaderd_enable=YES
		    service diablo start
		"service diablo check" runs the checks without starting.
		Settings in rc.conf: diablo_ctl (path of diablo-ctl) and
		diablo_flags (its options, for example "-f /news/diablo-ctl.conf").

  smf/diablo.xml, dreaderd.xml
		illumos and SmartOS: edit the paths if needed, then
		    svccfg import diablo.xml
		    svcadm enable site/diablo
		The daemons run in the service's contract: when they all die,
		SMF restarts the service.

  sysv/diablo, dreaderd
		SysV init (Debian without systemd, Devuan, old systems): copy to
		/etc/init.d, chmod 755, then update-rc.d diablo defaults (or
		your system's tool).  Settings in /etc/default/diablo:
		DIABLO_CTL and DIABLO_CTL_OPTS.

Order: on a machine with both, diablo starts first and dreaderd stops first (the
reader may fetch articles from the feeder).

CHECKS BEFORE A START.  Every start through these wrappers goes through
"diablo-ctl start", which first runs checks and refuses to start a daemon when
one fails; they are on by default (DIABLO_PREFLIGHT in diablo-ctl.conf).  The
service then fails with the reasons in its log (journalctl -u dreaderd; the
SMF log under /var/svc/log; /var/log/messages or the console for rc.d and
SysV).  Each daemon is checked and started on its own: a failed check
prevents that daemon from starting, the other daemons requested are still
tried.  The checks:

  mounts	every mount point of DIABLO_MOUNTS is mounted (a spool or a RAM
		disk that did not mount must not be started over an empty
		directory).  Off while DIABLO_MOUNTS is empty.
  active file	the active file the daemon uses (dactive.kp: dreaderd always,
		diablo only with "active" on or naming the file in diablo.config)
		exists, and is not older than DIABLO_ACTIVE_MAXAGE hours when
		that is set.
  unclean stop	the pid file is left, no process runs, and the daemon's log
		has not changed for DIABLO_MAX_DOWNTIME minutes (off unless set).
  history	not a failure when missing: diablo makes a new one, which is
		right for a new site.  A note is printed only when a snapshot
		(dhistory.snp or .old) is there; DIABLO_REQUIRE_HISTORY="yes"
		makes a missing history a failure.

install-portable.sh creates an empty active file, so a standard install passes
the check.  At a site set up by hand the file may be missing, and dreaderd (or
diablo with "active" on) will not start: create the file (README.READER:
dsyncgroups -a from an active file server, or the KP tools), or start once by
hand with "diablo-ctl -F start dreaderd" (-F skips the checks for that start
only).  On an existing site a refused start means something is wrong (a file
system that is not mounted, an active file that has gone stale): the lines
printed say what.  Put DIABLO_PREFLIGHT="no" into diablo-ctl.conf to get the old behaviour
of starting whatever the state.

"diablo-ctl check" (or "service diablo check" on FreeBSD) runs the checks and
starts nothing, for a cron job or before a reboot.  On illumos, whose find has
no -mmin, the two age limits are rounded up to whole days.
